==English==

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} originally, a lively and fast-paced dance; by extension, any quick and uneven movement.
#* '''1640&ndash;1687''', Charles Cotton, in his burlesque of Virgil:
#*: Bounce cry the port-holes, out they fly<br>And make the world dance '''Barnaby'''.
#* {{quote-text|year=1985|author=Gregory Sass|title=Redcoat|page=28|passage=It was a regular '''Barnaby''' dance; I'd never seen anyone move so quickly. Before the culprit bolted out the door into the night,}}
#* {{quote-text|year=1996|author=Jo Ann Ferguson|title=Miss Charity's Case|page=182|passage="Owell gave me this. Said to get it to you in a '''Barnaby''' dance. 'Ere it is." His tongue scraped across his lower lip as he stared at Charity.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=2009|author=Julia Golding|title=The Diamond of Drury Lane|page=434|passage=We were now doing a strange sort of '''Barnaby''' dance: shuffling to and fro as I blocked his attempts to set off in pursuit.}}

